    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. The keyboard navigation part is a big one for us. We've had some issues where users seem to get stuck—like they press tab and then the focus just vanishes, or it jumps to a random part of the screen that's not even in the main flow. It's really disruptive for the transaction process. Any specific technical patterns or ARIA implementations we should use to prevent those kinds of jumps or \"lost focus\" scenarios?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those \"lost focus\" scenarios can be frustrating, like a mischievous imp hiding your keys and making you search for them everywhere. To prevent those kinds of jumps, you can use a few technical patterns and ARIA implementations. \n\nFirst, make sure to use the `tabindex` attribute to set the initial focus on the correct element, and then use the `aria-hidden` attribute to hide elements that shouldn't be focused on. Think of it like setting a trap for the imp – you're guiding the user's focus to the correct spot.\n\nFor example, you can use `aria-hidden=\"true\"` on elements that shouldn't be focused on, like a loading animation or a modal dialog. This will help the screen reader and keyboard navigator understand that those elements are not interactive.\n\nAnother technique is to use the `aria-atomic` attribute on elements that should be updated atomically when the user navigates away from them. This will help the screen reader and keyboard navigator understand that the element's content should be updated in a single step, rather than jumping around.\n\nIn terms of specific patterns, you can use the \"tab order\" pattern to set the focus on interactive elements in a specific order. This can be achieved using the `tab",
      "source": "target",
